{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e","id":810975,"title":"Power Tiers in 2E?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-17T22:35:43-08:00","id":810975,"title":"Power Tiers in 2E?","content":"So I'm aware that white room comparisons of splats are fairly unhelpful and miss a lot of the point. Every supernatural is terrifying in their natural habitat and significantly less so on another's territory. And it's hard to make a comparison of raw power when power is ultimately relative (A werewolf can tear a vampire apart in seconds in a fight, but if he looks in that vampire's eyes once, he can probably kiss his free will goodbye, for example)<br /> <br /> That having been said, there was a general consensus in 1e that the earlier splats were significantly less powerful than the ones who came after them. Most people, from what I saw, ranked the supernaturals accordingly.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> 1. Vampires/Werewolves- superior to humans, but overall far too weak, especially the werewolves (Note that that last statement is based entirely on hearsay. I never played a werewolf game until 2e came out.)<br /> <br /> 2. Changelings/Prometheans- Individual contracts overall weaker than disciplines or gifts, but they're cheap and cover a wide variety of different situations. Between catches, pledges, tale crafting, and other stuff changelings were really good at stacking the deck in their favor. Promethean is a bit of a toss-up. Some people tended to put changelings and prometheans together in the same tier, but others ranked prometheans a bit higher in terms of being just plain impossible to kill.<br /> <br /> 3. Sin-Eaters- Crazy death wizards, basically. Boneyard by itself could solve pretty much and challenge thrown at a player fairly easily. Even without it, Sin-Eaters were crazy powerful compared to other splats, limited only by how hard it was for them to refuel.<br /> <br /> 4. Mummies/Mages- Another toss-up. Mages are touted as the absolute kings of CofD, but that's mainly just because Mummy never got the kind of love Mage did. A newly arisen mummy, of course, is pretty much the scariest fucking thing in existence that a player would generally be able to play, and whether you put these guys on the same tier basically came down to how many points you thought mummy lost for getting weaker over time, compared to the reality-warping powers of older mages. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Again, these, for the most part, aren't my opinions, just a sense I got of the general consensus.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Of course the general consensus is also that vampires and werewolves both got major upgrades with the shift to 2e. And a few other splats are of course undergoing some major overhauls as well. <br /> <br /> <br /> So my question is what is the general consensus now? Are vampires and werewolves still seen as way too weak? Based on the teasers and spoilers for the lines that are coming out, do the other lines seem poised to move up or down on the ladder? Any other comments or insights?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10984-neckbeard-the-pirate","name":"Neckbeard the Pirate","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-17T23:39:54-08:00","id":810996,"title":"","content":"My contribution to the consensus: you had it right in your first paragraph.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T00:01:23-08:00","id":811008,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neckbeard the Pirate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e#post810975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I'm aware that white room comparisons of splats are fairly unhelpful and miss a lot of the point. Every supernatural is terrifying in their natural habitat and significantly less so on another's territory.</div> </div> </div> </div>The specific changes made to 2nd Edition, actually increase this significantly in my opinion. Back in 1st Edition, it was a lot easier to compare the different splats for power level but we've been given a lot more apple vs orange comparisons when it comes to 2nd Edition.<br /> <br /> However long story short, Werewolves and Vampires have been given a significant power boost but only in specific areas related to their themes. Judging from the dev. notes Mages have had their abilities significantly expanded, however overall will probably end up a little weaker as the previous mechanical loopholes and exploits are being closed and the game is a lot more clear and specific about costs and downsides to powerful spells.<br /> <br /> Overall I'd say the power gap has been reduced between these three supernaturals, however this is only half because of changes to power level. The other half is because comparisons are a lot less meaningful now than they were in 1E.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6748-michael-kenner","name":"Michael Kenner","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6748_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T01:04:41-08:00","id":811031,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael Kenner</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811008#post811008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> However long story short, Werewolves and Vampires have been given a significant power boost but *only in specific areas related to their themes*. Judging from the dev. notes Mages have had their abilities significantly expanded, however overall will probably end up a little weaker as the previous mechanical loopholes and exploits are being closed and the game is a lot more clear and specific about costs and downsides to powerful spells.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that's entirely the case though. While I'm utterly unfamiliar with 1e werewolf, I can say that from 1st to 2nd edition, vampires became hugely more powerful in terms of their physical abilities. While they still don't match werewolves in that arena, it doesn't change the fact that they've gotten a huge boost in that particular arena, to the point where a really combat-oriented vampire, while not quite gelling with the usual theme of the game, is a viable character now, compared to 1e where most of the disciplines designed to make you better at fighting were often at best ineffectual and at worst impeded you more than if you didn't have them at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10984-neckbeard-the-pirate","name":"Neckbeard the Pirate","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T01:11:23-08:00","id":811034,"title":"","content":"I'll echo Dataweaver. Comparisons like this are utterly pointless, and if anything do more harm to crossover than good. The very idea that splats need to be compared this way is, honestly, damn silly. I mean, think about it: no one makes threads breaking down power balance between different bloodlines, or which Order packs the most mechanical punch, but for some reason when it comes to the different splats there must be tiers.<br /> <br /> Practically speaking, I believe there is only one tiering that is actually semi-useful: The 'danger posed by normal people' tiering, which breaks down how much mundane, run of the mill humans endanger a splat, and goes something like this:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><b>Tier 1 (Very Dangerous): </b><i>Vampire / Promethean - </i>Both splats live in constant fear of being outed to the general public, and with the knowledge that their reveal would most certainly result in a witch hunt.<br /> <br /> <b>Tier 2 (Moderately Dangerous): </b>Werewolf / Mummy - Normal humans are no match for characters at this tier, but will still see ample reason to take action against them. Both splats have mechanics for protecting their 'masquerade', but get enough normal humans together and they still offer up severe complications, should they get wind of you.<br /> <br /> <b>Tier 3 (Mildly Dangerous): </b><i>Changeling / Beast / Sin-Eaters</i> - At this tier it is almost impossible for a normal human to discern the supernatural creature, and even if they do the splats have easy outs, or fairly good chances of convincing the humans they are not a threat. (Werewolf would probably be here, if they weren't prone to Death Rage)<br /> <br /> <b>Tier 4 (Tertiary Danger): </b><i>Mage / Demon </i>- Humans pose no direct threat, complicating these splats lives only through the indirect intervention of higher powers (the Abyss and the God-Machine). The splats appear perfectly human, have powers that bulldose bystanders, and 99% of people either cannot perceive or forget all supernatural interaction with them.</div>One might be tempted to take this list as a tiering of actual power, but that would be a mistake. It really only applies in games where average people feature heavily. If your setting instead involves, say, a lot of ghosts and spirits the tiers would look very different.<br /> <br /> TL;DR - Tiers are pointless.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","user":"PenDragon","date":". 01-19-2016, 03:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T02:11:42-08:00","id":811043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neckbeard the Pirate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811031#post811031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think that's entirely the case though. While I'm utterly unfamiliar with 1e werewolf, I can say that from 1st to 2nd edition, vampires became hugely more powerful in terms of their physical abilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't get the problem, an improved capacity for physical violence is entirely within the themes of Vampire. It's not the primary theme, but much like werewolf it is a game about ultimate predators and so violent confrontation and death comes with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6748-michael-kenner","name":"Michael Kenner","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6748_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T06:44:07-08:00","id":811088,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neckbeard the Pirate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e#post810975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I'm aware that white room comparisons of splats are fairly unhelpful and miss a lot of the point.</div> </div> </div> </div> Yep. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Every supernatural is terrifying in their natural habitat and significantly less so on another's territory. And it's hard to make a comparison of raw power when power is ultimately relative (A werewolf can tear a vampire apart in seconds in a fight, but if he looks in that vampire's eyes once, he can probably kiss his free will goodbye, for example) </div> </div> </div> You didn't take it far enough. Its not so much &quot;on their territory&quot; since controllign territory is the werewolf's specialty. Its that every game has its focus, and is generally unbeatable in that focus. Vampires win at mind control. Werewolves at the hunt. Mages at studying, etc.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neckbeard the Pirate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e#post810975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That having been said, there was a general consensus in 1e that the earlier splats were significantly less powerful than the ones who came after them.</div> </div> </div> </div> Its called power creep. Even inside vampire, werewolf, and mage, you could see later stuff significantly more powerful than the core books. The CofD is too new<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So my question is what is the general consensus now? Are vampires and werewolves still seen as way too weak? Based on the teasers and spoilers for the lines that are coming out, do the other lines seem poised to move up or down on the ladder? Any other comments or insights? </div> </div> </div> We have THREE games out. Three. Vampire, werewolf, demon. We have most of Beast in our hands, but even that the finalized version. We haven't seen the Arcana, Transmutations, or Contracts in enough detail to being to compare anything. <br /> <br /> The general consensus is that fan wankery over who's better is nothing more than fan boys trying to prove their favorite supernatural is better than anyone else. It generally comes down to the fan boys for each side making up house rules and extrapolating rules to things they think should be included to make their splat better, and treating everyone else like idiots. Give the favorite all the advantages, make the others have disadvantages, and no crap one side will look better.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T06:53:31-08:00","id":811090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811034#post811034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">., or which Order packs the most mechanical punch</div> </div> </div> </div> That's because its clear that the Arrows and Mysterium were winning in 1e. And the FC is the underdog. Well, Seers and Banishers were winning in 1e, but they're NPC material, so they don't count.<br /> <br /> For 2e, I'm still pissed about the FC benefits being so easily rendered moot!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T08:48:05-08:00","id":811145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811034#post811034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> TL;DR - Tiers are pointless.</div> </div> </div> </div>On one level, I agree. On another I don't.<br /> <br /> The splats are not all equal to one another. They aren't supposed to be. The designers themselves have said the idea of &quot;balancing&quot; them is dumb. Splats are as powerful or as weak as they come in their books and the dev's are not shy about the fact that they haven't tried to keep them all equal and fair with one another.<br /> <br /> Which is a good thing, because it makes the world an organic one which doesn't feel overly controlled or contrived.<br /> <br /> But I feel like asking the question &quot;Well, then which ones *are* more powerful than the others?&quot; is fair, when we have already admitted that they are not all equal.<br /> <br /> <br /> I do like your tier based on safety from mortals, but I don't feel like it's the only useful one. I've always thought of defining the power of a splat based on a literal definition of power: the general ability to affect change in one's environment and accomplish one's goals swiftly and easily, not merely to resist the environment's effects, and on that basis, I'd personally rank the splats more.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> 1. Mages/Demons- Literally rewrite reality at will. Mages moreso in that regard, but Demons are better at improvising. They're limited less by what they're able to do and more by the consequences of doing it too overtly, but they can still afford to be overt in a pinch.<br /> <br /> 2. Vampires- Blood bond, ghouls, various mind control disciplines give a level of control over mortal society that most splats can't compete with. A vamp's zone of influence can extend far outside of himself and he can change entire groups of people's lives without ever being in the same neighborhood as them. Maybe the same city. He's not great at any change he can't affect by manipulating mortal society, but his sheer ability to do that is staggering, and a clever vamp can find a lot of ways to use mortals to solve his problems.<br /> <br /> 3. Changelings- Not as good as mind control or reality-bending, but guile and trickery goes a long way, and their contracts generally give them a wide array of abilities.<br /> <br /> 4. Werewolves- Really good at smashing, maiming, or destroying whatever's currently in front of them. Other than that, fairly limited. Can still produce farther reaching changes by being selective about who/what/when they smash, and their connection to the hisil gives them a unique ability to cultivate desired changes over time, but they can't do it as fast or directly as other splats.<br /> <br /> 5. Prometheans- Most of their best abilities are designed around just surviving whatever happens to be thrown at them. They're fairly limited in their ability to actually affect their environment, unless their desired goal for said environment is to ruin it, in which case they just have to set up camp for a few weeks and wait for their wasteland to take effect.<br /> <br /> That's not based on personal preference mind you. Werewolves are probably my favorite out of all the splats, but their bag of tricks is still fairly limited, which is a lot of why I like them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10984-neckbeard-the-pirate","name":"Neckbeard the Pirate","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T09:09:55-08:00","id":811152,"title":"","content":"Werewolves' bag of tricks seems pretty limited, until you remember fetishes. Then you realise that with prep time a werewolf can manage pretty much <i>anything. </i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T09:30:45-08:00","id":811163,"title":"","content":"A lot of Promethean powers are designed with the idea of surviving, avoiding notice, or managing the Promethean condition, I feel. However, there are some categories that can enable you to do some pretty impressive things. Vitality, Metamorphosis, and Corporeum, for example, can let you do more or less what Vigor, Protean, and Resilience does, plus a little extra. Where Promethean really shines, I think, is in the Alchemicus, Vulcanus, Argentum, and Contamination transmutation classes. The two powers that really allow a Promethean some really serious damage are Wrath of the Hecatoncarie (or Strike the Gates of Tartarus, I forget which has the higher dots) or Call Firestorm. One causes an earthquake, one causes a Firestorm, and Firestorms are hardly low-class supernatural weirdness in terms of their ability to wreck stuff.<br /> <br /> I honestly can't wait for Promethean 2E, since I'm fairly certain Transmutations are going to look <i>very </i>different than they used to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T09:56:40-08:00","id":811173,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811152#post811152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Werewolves' bag of tricks seems pretty limited, until you remember fetishes. Then you realise that with prep time a werewolf can manage pretty much <i>anything. </i></div> </div> </div> </div>That's true! I forgot fetishes. That does increase their versatility quite a bit, though they do somewhat require the uratha to know a bit ahead of time what they're going to be dealing with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10984-neckbeard-the-pirate","name":"Neckbeard the Pirate","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T10:21:36-08:00","id":811194,"title":"","content":"Also, while Werewolves' ability to affect change <i>is</i> fairly focused on change through destruction, it would be highly inaccurate to say only whatever is currently in front of them. For one thing, in 2e they have just as many powers to help them find and get to the things they want to destroy as they have powers to help them actually destroy, if not more. They're hunters first and warriors second. Also, have you seen the Gift of the Elementals and the Gift of Weather? werewolves have an easier time blowing up cities or other large areas that pretty much any other splat besides maybe mummies. Powerful mages can accomplish similar large-scale destruction, but the resulting Paradox could be catastrophic, whereas a starting werewolf character can easily create natural disasters with little to no risk to herself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 01-18-2016, 01:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T10:54:00-08:00","id":811216,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neckbeard the Pirate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811145#post811145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I feel like asking the question &quot;Well, then which ones *are* more powerful than the others?&quot; is fair, when we have already admitted that they are not all equal.</div> </div> </div> </div> Its not a fair question, because, unlike D&amp;D, the CofD doesn't have a built in rubric for what &quot;powerful&quot; means. Is an Elodoth Iron Master socialite &quot;less powerful&quot; than a Messanger Tempter socialite? What's the standard we're using to measure? How about we compare an Irraka Bone Shadow to a Mehket Accolyte. Who's &quot;more powerful.&quot; The wolves have access to spirits and fetishes, who could do anything in theory... but blood magic can likewise do anything in theory in return and has access to the strix. Potentially cast anything with swarms of entities that can have a struck deal. Shall we compare a Destroyer Sabatour to a Predator Eshmaki, see who does more damage before the angels come calling? Now, lets start comparing the Carthian Daeva socialite to the Rahu Blood Talon. Who is &quot;stronger&quot; when one is focused on interacting with humanity and the other on fighting? What about a Guardian Paranoid? They're all being super spies and manipulating time to protecting their loved ones. Are we comparing them to a Nightmare-focused Beast that specializes in supernatural contacts?<br /> <br /> The heart of D&amp;D is still a war game. Combat performance, followed by exploration abilities, is something that's easy to compare. In the CofD, we have so many different stanards to measure with. Its like trying to compare the Forgotten Realms to Dark Suns, Eberron, and some home brew low magic world all at once. Sure, you can play with all of them at once. But its a mess with different goals, standards, and needs. <br /> <br /> Generally speaking? These conversations come down to two things - white room combats, which are unrealistic even for a game and don't account for too many variables, or a &quot;I can do anything better than you!&quot; pout fest of bad rules lawyering and munchkin rule twisting. Neither of these are &quot;fair.&quot; They're unrealistic and baseless. You get far enough into the game, any of them can acomplish anything. Therefore, they're all equal tier.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T11:20:28-08:00","id":811227,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811216#post811216\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You get far enough into the game, any of them can acomplish anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not technically true. Demons can raise dead people back to life better/easier than Mummies and Sin-Eaters (thus far). Werewolves can heal from harm easier than everyone (at least long term). There <i>are</i> instances where X is better than Y at something. You may need to get very specific into individual members of the splats and criteria, but it is possible to do comparisons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T11:20:28-08:00","id":811228,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure Archmages take the top spot though, provided they still exist in 2E.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T11:53:38-08:00","id":811243,"title":"","content":"Archmages are an outlier, and should not be counted for anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","name":"Blakemikizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10820_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T12:00:48-08:00","id":811246,"title":"","content":"If you're counting Archmages, you may as well count the Gentry. They're essentially the 'Cosmic Tier' splat for Changeling.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T12:43:58-08:00","id":811266,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neckbeard the Pirate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811145#post811145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I feel like asking the question &quot;Well, then which ones *are* more powerful than the others?&quot; is fair, when we have already admitted that they are not all equal.<br /> <br /> I do like your tier based on safety from mortals, but I don't feel like it's the only useful one. I've always thought of defining the power of a splat based on a literal definition of power: the general ability to affect change in one's environment and accomplish one's goals swiftly and easily, not merely to resist the environment's effects, and on that basis, I'd personally rank the splats more.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, I'll admit I was overly critical in that post. And you are right, Tiers are not <i>entirely</i> pointless. For instance, if one is attempting to gauge which splat has the most utilitarian, high-speed problem solving potential then Mages and Demons are going to <b>dominate</b> the list. Because power and large scale problem solving are core to those splats themes. Similarly if you are trying to gauge which splats will do best in a D&amp;D style dungeon crawl then Werewolves are going to place very highly. But here's this tricky thing, you can make all these assumptions, and construct a game accordingly and have them all be wrong. Case-and-point, I ran a game with Prometheans, Vampires, Changelings, Sin-Eaters, Shapeshifters, Mad Geniuses, and Mages. Which player characters came out appearing the most 'powerful'? The Purified Immortal and run-of-the-mill Hunter, neither of which would have stood higher than the lowest rung of a tier list. Never the less they were dominating forces, not because of their powers, but because of their personalities, place in the story, and least of all the person playing them.<br /> <br /> And that's really where tier lists fall short, they are an inhuman metric for human characters. They cannot take into account the character's personality, or the player's ingenuity. A tier list wont tell you that player 'Bob' struggles when presented with too many options, and thus will play a shit Mage. Or that 'Susan' struggles to grasp complex systems, but will shine with a simple, straight forward character. Nor does a tier list care that your character is trying to keep his Wisdom high, or hates his Geist, or is an impulsive twit. Tier lists don't account for story or character, and in a setting that prioritizes <i>storytelling flawed people</i> that is a humongous failing.<br /> <br /> Even still, Tiers do have use as a planning mechanism in the formative stages of a game. If you are the sort who likes lists (I love lists) then it may be genuinely valuable to create tiers for the various major challenges just to get a feel for which splats will have the opportunity to shine. Of course that should ideally account for the players, characters, and general scale of the game. They also serve a degree of value helping storytellers very new to the system understand, at a glance, some of the difficulties other first time (or more) storytellers have had with crossover, and maybe prompt them to give their party balance more consideration.<br /> <br /> But again, for all the limited utility they offer, tiers bear so little weight next to the people involved that they generally serve as no more than a source of bickering argument or general entertainment. Hence my initial, extra negative response.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T12:56:24-08:00","id":811271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811194#post811194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, while Werewolves' ability to affect change <i>is</i> fairly focused on change through destruction, it would be highly inaccurate to say only whatever is currently in front of them. For one thing, in 2e they have just as many powers to help them find and get to the things they want to destroy as they have powers to help them actually destroy, if not more. They're hunters first and warriors second. Also, have you seen the Gift of the Elementals and the Gift of Weather? werewolves have an easier time blowing up cities or other large areas that pretty much any other splat besides maybe mummies. Powerful mages can accomplish similar large-scale destruction, but the resulting Paradox could be catastrophic, whereas a starting werewolf character can easily create natural disasters with little to no risk to herself.</div> </div> </div> </div>I will definitely concede that they're primarily hunters, and that they do have more powers than just slaughtering things. I probably focused in on that aspect of them too closely, and I am definitely reconsidering my appraisal of them.<br /> <br /> But I think you might be overestimating the destructive capabilities of weather and elementals. Weather is mainly a lot of utility powers, and the closest it gets to large scale destruction is conjuring a particularly nasty thunderstorm. Definitely bad for anyone inside of it, but it's not great for clearing land as much as it is highly restricting the movements of whoever's trapped inside of it. Elements does have the cataclysm facet and that is large-scale destruction. But it's large scale destruction in an area of 10 square miles at most. That's not exactly &quot;small&quot; but it sounds a lot bigger than it is. That's, on average, the amount of land you cover in 10 minutes of driving in any given direction. Sizable, but not really an entire city. Maybe a small town.<br /> <br /> And even then you have to ask the question &quot;What is it good for?&quot; Yeah, you can blow up 10 square miles (Probably 2-4 out of character gen), and it's insanely useful when you need 10 square miles blown up. But it's not a very versatile power. Any time you have to worry about collateral damage, you can't really use it. Any problem that can't be solved by abject destruction, it's not really useful. Compare the strength gift. The simplest thing there, adding your purity to your strength? That's not just fighting and destroying. Running, jumping, lifting, all benefit from it. There's a lot of versatility in that one power. Honestly, for elements, I'd prefer just getting one of the influence powers. Cataclysm will come up like... once in a werewolf's career, but you'll get a lot of mileage out of the ability to manipulate earth or air.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10984-neckbeard-the-pirate","name":"Neckbeard the Pirate","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T12:59:58-08:00","id":811274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811266#post811266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, I'll admit I was overly critical in that post. And you are right, Tiers are not <i>entirely</i> pointless. For instance, if one is attempting to gauge which splat has the most utilitarian, high-speed problem solving potential then Mages and Demons are going to <b>dominate</b> the list. Because power and large scale problem solving are core to those splats themes. Similarly if you are trying to gauge which splats will do best in a D&amp;D style dungeon crawl then Werewolves are going to place very highly. But here's this tricky thing, you can make all these assumptions, and construct a game accordingly and have them all be wrong. Case-and-point, I ran a game with Prometheans, Vampires, Changelings, Sin-Eaters, Shapeshifters, Mad Geniuses, and Mages. Which player characters came out appearing the most 'powerful'? The Purified Immortal and run-of-the-mill Hunter, neither of which would have stood higher than the lowest rung of a tier list. Never the less they were dominating forces, not because of their powers, but because of their personalities, place in the story, and least of all the person playing them.<br /> <br /> And that's really where tier lists fall short, they are an inhuman metric for human characters. They cannot take into account the character's personality, or the player's ingenuity. A tier list wont tell you that player 'Bob' struggles when presented with too many options, and thus will play a shit Mage. Or that 'Susan' struggles to grasp complex systems, but will shine with a simple, straight forward character. Nor does a tier list care that your character is trying to keep his Wisdom high, or hates his Geist, or is an impulsive twit. Tier lists don't account for story or character, and in a setting that prioritizes <i>storytelling flawed people</i> that is a humongous failing.<br /> <br /> Even still, Tiers do have use as a planning mechanism in the formative stages of a game. If you are the sort who likes lists (I love lists) then it may be genuinely valuable to create tiers for the various major challenges just to get a feel for which splats will have the opportunity to shine. Of course that should ideally account for the players, characters, and general scale of the game. They also serve a degree of value helping storytellers very new to the system understand, at a glance, some of the difficulties other first time (or more) storytellers have had with crossover, and maybe prompt them to give their party balance more consideration.<br /> <br /> But again, for all the limited utility they offer, tiers bear so little weight next to the people involved that they generally serve as no more than a source of bickering argument or general entertainment. Hence my initial, extra negative response.</div> </div> </div> </div>Believe me, I understand. Like I said at the beginning, I'm totally aware that general rankings of different splats are going to fall apart when you get into individual player characters. But I think it's helpful to have a general idea of what a given splat is capable of. I was also mainly just curious what people's opinions were going into 2e, since there seemed to be such a major agreement about which splats did and didn't have problems compared to the others.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, no worries about the critical thing. Getting passionate about a subject is understandable, especially when it's a topic that may have sparked flames in the past.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10984-neckbeard-the-pirate","name":"Neckbeard the Pirate","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T13:10:39-08:00","id":811283,"title":"","content":"An &quot;all purpose&quot; tier list is too vague to be useful. What *can* be useful is breaking the tiers up into categories, examining each splat within that category, and ranking them. Not only is that the appropriate amount of fan wanking, but newer players could get an idea of how a splat tends to approach x problems.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T13:23:11-08:00","id":811289,"title":"","content":"Categories should probably be...<br /> <br /> Combat<br /> Infiltration<br /> Influencing Individuals<br /> Influencing Groups<br /> Gathering Information<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T13:24:35-08:00","id":811290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neckbeard the Pirate</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811271#post811271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I will definitely concede that they're primarily hunters, and that they do have more powers than just slaughtering things. I probably focused in on that aspect of them too closely, and I am definitely reconsidering my appraisal of them.<br /> <br /> But I think you might be overestimating the destructive capabilities of weather and elementals. Weather is mainly a lot of utility powers, and the closest it gets to large scale destruction is conjuring a particularly nasty thunderstorm. Definitely bad for anyone inside of it, but it's not great for clearing land as much as it is highly restricting the movements of whoever's trapped inside of it. Elements does have the cataclysm facet and that is large-scale destruction. But it's large scale destruction in an area of 10 square miles at most. That's not exactly &amp;quot;small&amp;quot; but it sounds a lot bigger than it is. That's, on average, the amount of land you cover in 10 minutes of driving in any given direction. Sizable, but not really an entire city. Maybe a small town.<br /> <br /> And even then you have to ask the question &amp;quot;What is it good for?&amp;quot; Yeah, you can blow up 10 square miles (Probably 2-4 out of character gen), and it's insanely useful when you need 10 square miles blown up. But it's not a very versatile power. Any time you have to worry about collateral damage, you can't really use it. Any problem that can't be solved by abject destruction, it's not really useful. Compare the strength gift. The simplest thing there, adding your purity to your strength? That's not just fighting and destroying. Running, jumping, lifting, all benefit from it. There's a lot of versatility in that one power. Honestly, for elements, I'd prefer just getting one of the influence powers. Cataclysm will come up like... once in a werewolf's career, but you'll get a lot of mileage out of the ability to manipulate earth or air.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never said it was a versatile power. But it is one of the most effective powers in all of CofD for destroying everything in a large area, which is distinctly a different thing than &quot;smashing whatever is right in front of them&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T13:29:48-08:00","id":811295,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811227#post811227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> There <i>are</i> instances where X is better than Y at something.</div> </div> </div> </div> I am talking about generalities. Every game has its specialty, yes. But when we get to &quot;mages and spirits can do anything!&quot; It's important to note the same is true for everyone - in theory, anything is possible. In reality, not so much. But all that relies on specific builds, which is silly to discuss. Thus, my point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T13:58:37-08:00","id":811307,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SoulGambit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811289#post811289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Categories should probably be...<br /> <br /> Combat<br /> Infiltration<br /> Influencing Individuals<br /> Influencing Groups<br /> Gathering Information</div> </div> </div> </div> Even that is pretty weak measure. Irraka are too different from Mehket, from Wheels, from.. Mastigos? Eshmaki?<br /> <br /> What about an Invictus retainer Swarm versus a Trumpet gunner versus Nightmare focused social Beast? <br /> <br /> Even Carthean versus Elodoth Iron Master versus Tempted versus Silver Ladder versus Spring all do their social group full massively differently. An Elodoth on the social hunt is.scary, but less.so on casual use. <br /> <br /> I honestly think that the best.way to.look at it is individual splat specialties. Mind control, the hunt, solving mysteries, dreams and stories, surviving. Etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T14:21:16-08:00","id":811320,"title":"","content":"<br /> If &quot;infiltration&quot; is the goal, then the questions are &quot;How well does xyz splat get from point A to point B without being noticed? What does that cost in terms of resources? What security would be effective against what xyz splat can do?&quot; Yes, an irraka will do this different from a mekhet. Listing how and where each approach shines is the value of such a list.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1157,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","user":"SoulGambit","date":". 01-18-2016, 05:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T14:52:50-08:00","id":811336,"title":"","content":"If we want a tier list split into categories, might I suggest:<br /> -Versatility, how many things than a starting character plus X Experiences do out of the box? Mages and Demons are going to take the A tier, but I'm curious where others get placed.<br /> -Scope, how many people/how large an area can a starting character plus X experiences effect? I think Werewolves, Demons and Maybe Vampires will win here, but I'm honestly not sure.<br /> -Effect, which can cause the effect with the most extreme consequences. I suggest limiting it to a single target so as not to cross over with scope.<br /> <br /> Add more as desired, but the point here is not saying 'Vampires are social beasts'. We KNOW that kindred are social beasts, their entire game is built around hiding in human society and interacting with a society of monsters, not being a social beast brings up the question of how do they manage, what might be interesting is ranking what each splat is second best at.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:blue\">Blue is sarcasm.</span><br /> <br /> If I suggestion I make contradicts in-setting metaphysics please ignore me, I probably brought in scientific ideas.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4507-twodsix","name":"TwoDSix","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"833"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T16:36:43-08:00","id":811371,"title":"","content":"The only purpose that tier lists that deal with the potency of a splat is in a single area. When deciding on safe and unsafe crossover games where vast disparities can cause issues with player participation. Beyond that it's a bit of fan wankery usually.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T16:42:08-08:00","id":811375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811228#post811228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm pretty sure Archmages take the top spot though, provided they still exist in 2E.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> They do. They're even described briefly in the 2e corebook.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T16:45:13-08:00","id":811377,"title":"","content":"Here are the tiers, from top to bottom:<br /> <br /> Those who can make the most of their characters abilities.<br /> Those who can't.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T17:30:29-08:00","id":811407,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SoulGambit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811320#post811320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If &quot;infiltration&quot; is the goal, then the questions are &quot;How well does xyz splat get from point A to point B without being noticed? What does that cost in terms of resources? What security would be effective against what xyz splat can do?&quot; Yes, an irraka will do this different from a mekhet. Listing how and where each approach shines is the value of such a list.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that they all infiltrate equally well. They just infiltrate differently. Lists are meaningless when everyone rates close enough that it's impossible to objectively rank them and favoritism/playstyle takes over as the primary deciding factor.<br /> <br /> Trying to rate in terms of resources is likewiset a trap. Vitae is, by design, easier to replenish than essence or aether, let alone Satiety. Satiety effectively caps at 3 in a pool, since one is going to want to focus on either High or Low for the Mission duration. <br /> <br /> Too many factors","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 01-18-2016, 08:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T17:38:25-08:00","id":811411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811407#post811407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is that they all infiltrate equally well. They just infiltrate differently. Lists are meaningless when everyone rates close enough that it's impossible to objectively rank them and favoritism/playstyle takes over as the primary deciding factor.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> We don't know that yet, since people haven't thought that through and posted their findings. Maybe they are all equally great. Even if they are all equal and it's meaningless, taking the effort to explore what options splats have regarding certain areas has in itself some meaning.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T17:56:54-08:00","id":811417,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811407#post811407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is that they all infiltrate equally well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Demons and Prometheans (1ed, at least) might beg to differ.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T18:01:14-08:00","id":811419,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811216#post811216\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Its not a fair question, because, unlike D&amp;D, the CofD doesn't have a built in rubric for what &quot;powerful&quot; means.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Incorrect. D&amp;D does <i>not</i> have a rubric for what most powerful means, at least no more so than the CofD. People <i>defined</i> what the tiers were meant to represent, as we are currently doing now, and then rated which classes were the best using that predefined standard. So tier lists are as valid here as they were there.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811407#post811407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is that they all infiltrate equally well. They just infiltrate differently. Lists are meaningless when everyone rates close enough that it's impossible to objectively rank them and favoritism/playstyle takes over as the primary deciding factor.<br /> <br /> Trying to rate in terms of resources is likewiset a trap. Vitae is, by design, easier to replenish than essence or aether, let alone Satiety. Satiety effectively caps at 3 in a pool, since one is going to want to focus on either High or Low for the Mission duration.<br /> <br /> Too many factors</div> </div> </div> </div>As SoulGambit said, we don't really know that since we haven't taken the time to explore/test that, which is how tier lists are formed. It is very rare that two things that work vastly differently handle the same set of tasks equally. While the difference might be relatively small, sometimes they aren't, and it is interesting (to some at least, myself included) to figure out where various things stand compared to one another.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 01-18-2016, 09:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T18:07:40-08:00","id":811423,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811419#post811419\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Incorrect. D&amp;D does <i>not</i> have a rubric for what most powerful means, at least no more so than the CofD.</div> </div> </div> </div>Have they taken Challenge Rating out of the game? It's been a few years and editions since I played D&amp;D.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6748-michael-kenner","name":"Michael Kenner","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6748_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T18:16:05-08:00","id":811430,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael Kenner</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811423#post811423\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Have they taken Challenge Rating out of the game? It's been a few years and editions since I played D&amp;D.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ha, Challenge Rating. Challenge Rating was a joke. There were some low Challenge Rating things that if thrown at even parties that well exceeded the bar would wipe the party and some things with extremely high CR that could easily be dealt with way below the indicated level. CR was so generally inaccurate I almost forgot it was a thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T18:39:57-08:00","id":811442,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811430#post811430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ha, Challenge Rating. Challenge Rating was a joke. There were some low Challenge Rating things that if thrown at even parties that well exceeded the bar would wipe the party and some things with extremely high CR that could easily be dealt with way below the indicated level. CR was so generally inaccurate I almost forgot it was a thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree, but that's not really the point. The point behind bringing up Challenge Rating, was that Dungeon's and Dragons had a preconceived notion of adventuring as a set of four challenges (or the equivalent) of approximately the Challenge Rating of your parties average ECL per in game day, This isn't some fan conception, this is straight from their DM advice in the encounter design chapter. Challenge Rating was built around the idea of a game of mechanical chance, simulating brave adventurers on heroic combat-oriented quests. The power level of your character in D&amp;D was your ability to meet that specific challenge.<br /> <br /> It's a lot harder to pin down what an &quot;adventure&quot; is in Chronicles of Darkness and thus it's harder to determine which characters are more able to complete one. It places Chronicles on a much more subjective assessment of power.<br /> <br /> <i><b>Edit: </b>Sorry, I realize now that I think about it that I'm a little out of date with my D&amp;D references for anyone else that plays that game as well. My example above was from the 3rd Edition of that game if anyone was confused.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6748,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6748-michael-kenner","user":"Michael Kenner","date":". 01-18-2016, 09:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6748-michael-kenner","name":"Michael Kenner","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6748_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T19:33:45-08:00","id":811477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811430#post811430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ha, Challenge Rating. Challenge Rating was a joke. There were some low Challenge Rating things that if thrown at even parties that well exceeded the bar would wipe the party and some things with extremely high CR that could easily be dealt with way below the indicated level. CR was so generally inaccurate I almost forgot it was a thing.</div> </div> </div> </div> Just because you don't like it doens't mean its not a concept baked into the game. There are other calculations, such as how many encounters a day one should run into, resource alotment, scaling of magic items, etc. You are ignoring information that does not agree with your view.<br /> <br /> Further, even in the fifth edition of the game, which has spent more time focusing on exploration and social interaction than many previous editions, the overwhelming majority of the game is still devoted to combat, and it is commonly accepted that you rate things based on combat performance. No matter your race/class/other combination, you are a character designed to engage in battles. Its a common point all PCs in D&amp;D share. Even if you didn't have official attempts at ranking from WotC, its inevitable that unofficial rankings would grow, because we all operate under the same rules and assumptions in D&amp;D. D&amp;D was born of war games, and still bears a number of traits that its origin provided.<br /> <br /> The closest equivalent in CofD involves using all the subsystems together - we have social maneuvering, combat, tracking/chases, investigation/research, stealth and potentially social-group-fu. We also have crafting/gardening and dreams as potential areas of influence. That's a lot of different directions you can take a character, and they all bleed over into each other in a tangled mess. Even if you wanted to take one area here (say, investigation) and rate all the different supernaturals, there's still no basis of comparison. Vampires specialize in mind control, werewolves the hunt, demons on spying. Each of those three will approach investigation through the appropriate lens, and all will reach the end in roughly the same shape. If you completely eliminate any and all humans/ephemeral entities specifically to remove any advantage a vampire might have, you are screwing over the vamp. On the other hand, if you have an open room with nowhere to hide, the Irraka is out of luck, ironically making a white room stealth challenge impossible for her. Mages have trouble dealing with unexpected twists in ways vamps and wolves do not - lack of preparation is an issue.<br /> <br /> Everything is simply too fluid to make an objective determination here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T20:01:37-08:00","id":811490,"title":"","content":"It has nothing to do with liking it or not. It was that it didn't work. Sure, it was designed to serve a purpose, but it utterly failed to live up to that purpose. D&amp;D <i>tried</i> to have something to measure powerbut the system they implemented didn't actually <i>work</i>. Regardless, even ignoring the fact that it didn't work, the Tier lists of D&amp;D (at least 3.5), which is what we were comparing this to in the first place, weren't even based around using CR, so that point is moot anyways.<br /> <br /> And while it is commonly accepted that things are based off of combat, there are still side mentions about how certain classes excel at other types of tasks. That's why you have multiple Tier lists for multiple tasks. There's no reason that just because you have a combat tier list you cannot also have a social tier list, or information gathering tier list. These things existed in D&amp;D and it wasn't like they were immeasurable concepts even then, and they certainly aren't right now, here, with the CofD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 01-18-2016, 11:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T20:43:02-08:00","id":811517,"title":"","content":"CR works much better in 5e. Just saying.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T20:50:47-08:00","id":811521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811517#post811517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">CR works much better in 5e. Just saying.</div> </div> </div> </div>Quite Likely. Though to be fair, at least from what I've seen, 5e is an <i>extremely</i> simple system. I don't know if they've released a lot more content and that's changed, but it was pretty sparse when I checked it out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T21:02:46-08:00","id":811525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811521#post811521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Quite Likely. Though to be fair, at least from what I've seen, 5e is an <i>extremely</i> simple system. I don't know if they've released a lot more content and that's changed, but it was pretty sparse when I checked it out.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's pretty minimalist, though there's somewhat of a sliding scale of complexity. More importantly to the CR system working, however, is the fact that the 5e DMG has much better advice on using CR and building encounters than the 3.5 DMG did. And, like, better advice in general.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T21:53:19-08:00","id":811545,"title":"","content":"Don't know why CR is what people are talking about since the main reason classes end up considered low tier in D&amp;D is if they can Only do combat. Combat is the worst way you can make a judgement on &quot;How powerful a class is&quot; in D&amp;D, for many of the same reasons as CofD. Every class in D&amp;D is designed so that you can be decent in combat, just like all the splats have combat abilities.<br /> <br /> People have said in this thread that a tier system doesn't make sense since there are multiple different forms of power and capabilities that each possesses and each are better at different niches.... except that is true in D&amp;D/PF and making a tier system isn't that difficult in it. I think there is a large amount of backlash against tiers in the CofD community because of things like &quot;which would beat which in a fight&quot; and white room scenario's and such, which can understandably leave a bad taste in everyones mouths, but seriously, tiers are not that terrible of a concept.<br /> <br /> Also, CR does work better in 5e. Though 3.5e didn't leave a high bar... even PF's CR system is much better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":8331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","user":"milo v3","date":". 01-19-2016, 01:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T01:20:13-08:00","id":811603,"title":"","content":"Getting back on topic: I don't have any proof of this, but I suspect that trying to rank the supernatural types based on how well they cope with a particular type of challenge will end up with a list of challenges at least as long as the list of supernatural types, with each type taking first place in at least one of the challenge rankings. <br /> <br /> Also keep in mind that a given challenge ranking might end up with a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partially_ordered_set\" target=\"_blank\">partial order</a> due to the inability to find a basis for comparison between two types that approach the challenge in fundamentally different ways. Indeed, I suspect that this will prove to be the rule rather than the exception. You might not even be able to get a clear \u201cbest\u201d (or \u201cworst\u201d), for that matter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 01-19-2016, 04:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T03:00:56-08:00","id":811634,"title":"","content":"Seconding Dataweaver yet again.<br /> <br /> Further more you are going to run into complications vis-a-vis the theoretical ideal and the practical example of a splat. Mages, for instance, are all but guaranteed to top nearly every category. This will come as no surprise to anyone who knows Mage well, it's a game where <u>power</u> is a key theme, and the general response to &quot;Can a Mage do that?&quot; is &quot;Yes, eleven different ways&quot;. Of course, in practical application this is miles from the truth, a real character won't have access to all Arcana, unfailing situational comprehension or a pure utilitarian personality, and that's not accounting for time, scenario and the player simply dropping the ball. But the theoretical Mage-Splat Mage does, and that's what the Tier list will reflect.<br /> <br /> Similarly, Vampires are likely to rank low in many categories where, in the context of a story, they would probably shine. Combat is a good example, Vampires pale in comparison to Werewolves in white room battles. We had a thread on it, it's not a good matchup...in the white room. Largely because the white room utterly neglects all the social manipulation, preparation, and sheer get-out-of-dodge potential the kindred possess. Throw in context to manipulate and suddenly Vampires will astonish you with their out of the box problem solving. Of course that's true for all splats (especially Demon, the unchained are ungodly good at out of the box problem solving), but that really only makes the tier problem worse.<br /> <br /> Never the less, if we are to engage in tier making I shall propose a few:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Which splats are best at getting elected president?</li> <li>Which splats are best at sleeping?</li> <li>Which splats are best at cleaning up the streets? (I mean literally cleaning them of dirt and litter, not crime. Ignore crime. Except littering, assuming that's a crime where you live.)</li> <li>Which splats are best at eating someone's Grandmother, putting on her cloths, and impersonating her when her granddaughter comes to visit?</li> </ol>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","user":"PenDragon","date":". 01-19-2016, 06:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T03:38:26-08:00","id":811650,"title":"","content":"1-Mage (so many ways to win or rig elecions)<br /> 2-Vampires (you don't get to out-nap a torpored elder)<br /> 3-Mage/Werewolf (Matter-Space and Cataclism Breath of air)<br /> 4-Werewolf (quite appropiate)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T04:12:31-08:00","id":811662,"title":"","content":"2. Mages are unarguably the worst. One of their antagonist groups are called <i>sleepers</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","name":"Xenesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1462_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T04:15:10-08:00","id":811663,"title":"","content":"and I disagree.. werewolf isn't that good on impersonating bit, I'd say vampire.. eat the granny (if we an interpret eating as draining her dry) use Obfuscate to look exactly like her <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> if we can't interpret it that way, than I'd say whoever's the best at impersonating others.. Since eating the granny is just the matter of killing her and stomaching it all..<br /> <br /> Oh oh.. would Demon's soul pacting count as eating the granny? If so.. Demon. <br /> <br /> Or... <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B5_EJSgV1u4SOFFJc3M5VmFDWVk?resourcekey=0-GEErNnDbGqlKYgoiuGausw&amp;usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Bloodline conversions</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IbeKfqhWuG8jPN9vpGmDo0VY9n9-4ejxvRzfJRoir4k/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My House rules</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/294-griautis","name":"Griautis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar294_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T05:51:53-08:00","id":811724,"title":"","content":"A werewolf could literally skin the granny and wear said skin as a perfect disguise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T05:55:00-08:00","id":811727,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811490#post811490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It has nothing to do with liking it or not. It was that it didn't work.</div> </div> </div> </div> One, this is an opinion, not fact. Two, it depends on edition, blanket statements are meaningless. Three, it doesn't matter if it doesn't work. What matters is that ranking power and ability is an innate part of D&amp;D. Saying otherwise is just being in denial.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811545#post811545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">People have said in this thread that a tier system doesn't make sense since there are multiple different forms of power and capabilities that each possesses and each are better at different niches.... except that is true in D&amp;D/PF and making a tier system isn't that difficult in it. I think there is a large amount of backlash against tiers in the CofD community because of things like &quot;which would beat which in a fight&quot; and white room scenario's and such, which can understandably leave a bad taste in everyones mouths, but seriously, tiers are not that terrible of a concept.</div> </div> </div> </div> Yes, it pretty much is a terrible concept. It makes sense in D&amp;D, because you have all these options to pick from when choosing a race and class. You have the options of picking wizard, sorcerer, warlock, bard, etc. Ranking serves a purpose there, because it informs meaningful choices, and helps prevent character overlap. Furthermore, ranking relies on specific builds. The overwhelming majority of games in the CofD aren't going to include a mix of different supernaturals, however, and the way characters are make it hard to nail down &quot;specific builds.&quot; Even games that do involve crossover characters tend to be less worried about balance, because the innate drawbacks of each supernatural (daylight restriction, essence needs, drives of the splats) tend to be magnified and cause issues. So what if the vampire can socialize better than anyone else when he has to be asleep during business hours? How good is a werewolf in a physical confrontation if you risk Death Rage and killing your entire group every single time?<br /> <br /> There is simply no justification for a tier system other than proving your favorite splat is &quot;better&quot; than another. Which is meaningless, because each game has its individual focus.<br /> <br /> ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> Playtesting and personal experience reported from different people have also found that personal understanding of the rules and individual creativity in the environment, as well as ST interpretation / biases of the games, have a huge impact on how things play out.<br /> <br /> Vampires excell at mind-control. If it relates to that in some way, vampires come out ahead.<br /> Werewolves excell at the hunt. This often includes chases, tracking and combat as secondary features.<br /> Mages are awesome at investigation and research. Studying mystries is there thing, scooby gang.<br /> Prometheans are survivors. Good luck killing one.<br /> Changelings are illusionists and talecrafters. Most every Contract had a temporary-dream-fading-away quality to them.<br /> Demons are spies. Spy games are their forte. Want to infiltrate somewhere? Your people for the job.<br /> Beasts are experts at dealing with other supernaturals.<br /> <br /> This above list, which focuses on the strengths of each individual game, is the only method I've seen that really &quot;rates&quot; the supernaturals in any meaninful way. Vampires always beat out anyone else at mind control. Even mages need Mind 4 just to open all doors in a social maneuver system. Vampires can do that with a vinculum as an innate ability, and that's not touching their Disciplines which often bypass the need for opening Doors in the first place. Vampire Predatory Auras also relate to magical mind-influence, where a werewolf's Aspect relates to hunting. Each of a werewolf's five forms somehow helps them with hunting down others.<br /> <br /> Werewolves are single handedly best at tracking - Auspex, Space magic, and Atavisms don't come close to what a werewolf's nose can pull off, let alone the right Gifts. Yes, I said Space mages lose to werewolves when it comes to tracking people. Sure, they could theoretically track someone... if they either were familiar with the person, or had the person's name and some part of their body for a working sympathetic connection; wolves aren't nearly so restricted. Mages need to investigate some first... which is only fair, because investigation is the Mage forte. Werewolf also gets more fighting merits than I've seen elsewhere - vampires get two, beasts get one, demons get... well, I suppose you could count the Advanced Form merit, kind of. Werewolves get at least eight, and eleven if you count Favored Form, Living Weapon and Fortified Form, giving a lot of variety and possibilities.<br /> <br /> Mages are investigators and researchers. They seek out mysteries to study. Almost everything revolves around information in some way. Low level Arcana? Built to reveal information. Mage Sight abilities? Reveal information. Sympathetic connection? Need information to track someone. Organization? The Lex Magica relies on studying precidents and established law, and that means hitting the books rather than being good at arguing. Orders are based on mystery cults, people seeking mysteries and the unexplained in the larger world. The Mysterium is a giant network of libraries across the world. People say that, in theory, a mage can do anything. Like everything else in mage, theory rarely plays out in reality. Because mages are only as strong as whatever they prepare, and preparation relies on information of what the day will bring.<br /> <br /> Demon is a spy game. Cover is their &quot;aura&quot; and lets them hide nearly perfectly from anyone or anything. Naturally undetectable, unable to be caught in a lie, and ability to change their faces and develop the necessary contacts or status to get into anywhere is an innate ability. That's not counting any embeds, exploits, or demon form abilities. Exposure is their weakness - start having people pry into your life, and it all comes undone. Demons seem to be the &quot;strongest&quot; supernatural until you realize that they have to keep everything under wraps, or they die. Even the famed ability to lie only passes cursory glances and mundane tells - abilities like mind reading can bypass to the truth easily, and you can bet the GM has psychics around.<br /> <br /> Beast is a game that revolves around dealing with other supernaturals, both as allies and antagonists. They literally become stronger for it, and the overwhelming majority of their abilities revolve around dealing with these others. Hunter is much the same - with a stronger emphasis on dealing with other supernaturals as antagonists. In many ways, these two games are two sides of the same exact coin.<br /> <br /> Prometheans are survivors. They're tough to kill, they can eat almost anything to no ill effect (seriously - I once had a Promethean that lived off <i>gasoline </i>with no ill effect), can go longer without sleep than others. Its been too long since I've played 1e, and 2e's not out, so I'll leave it at this.<br /> <br /> Changelings are illusionists and wish granters. The Masks, dreams, even Contracts tend to operate on a &quot;ephemeral dream that fades away&quot; principle. Even Contracts of the Forge, make things that fade away. Pledges are like wishes - it lasts as long as you follow the twisted logic of the Wyrd, and eveything can turn to nightmares. Unlike other oaths and similar abilities, pledges can be tricked into. Even Clarity reflects an inability to tell illusory fantasy from reality. Talecrafting is another thing that relates to illusions/dreams, because you're bringing these stories to life, forcing reality to conform to dream logic. It always amazed me how often people underestimated Changelings. No one can match changelings with making your wishes come true. Or twisting said wishes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> There, that's your ranking. Focus on your splat's strengths, and you're better for it. That's the best I've personally seen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T06:06:30-08:00","id":811733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811727#post811727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even the famed ability to lie only passes cursory glances and mundane tells - abilities like mind reading can bypass to the truth easily</div> </div> </div> </div>No, no they can't. Not even a power that tells you <i>whether a statement is true or not</i> will work on the unchained. It is <i>that</i> iron-clad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T06:50:16-08:00","id":811750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811733#post811733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, no they can't. Not even a power that tells you <i>whether a statement is true or not</i> will work on the unchained. It is <i>that</i> iron-clad.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Unchained aren't immune to mind-reading. There's a sample gadget and an Embed based around that specific fact.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T06:54:18-08:00","id":811756,"title":"","content":"You seem to be under the impression that what I just said was an interpretation and not an almost direct quote from the book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T07:02:09-08:00","id":811764,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811756#post811756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You seem to be under the impression that what I just said was an interpretation and not an almost direct quote from the book.</div> </div> </div> </div>You seem to be under the impression that the &quot;even by implication&quot; clause of spoofing's &quot;counter supernatural effects that would register the demon as nonhuman&quot; also applies to Liar's Tongue's &quot;demons' lies can register as true to supernatural detection.&quot;<br /> <br /> As the gadget notes, being a consummate liar doesn't stop invasive psychics from pulling secrets out of your head.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T07:18:33-08:00","id":811776,"title":"","content":"Ok, I just spent the last 20 minutes trying to find this damn Embed, where is it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T07:34:01-08:00","id":811788,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/810975-power-tiers-in-2e?p=811727#post811727\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There, that's your ranking. Focus on your splat's strengths, and you're better for it. That's the best I've personally seen.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Sure, you overlooked Sin-Eaters, Mummies, and hunters; but you demonstrated that the principle is sound. This is what I meant about there being at least as many challenges as types, with each type being best at <i>something</i> important; and a list of what each supernatural type is good at is <i>far</i> more valuable than an attempt to set up an overall hierarchy of best to worst.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T07:37:08-08:00","id":811791,"title":"","content":"Seriously, I know it exists, I remember reading it not too long ago, so <i>where the hell has it gone?</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T07:59:15-08:00","id":811800,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-ca